---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why is the internet slow in Pakistan today?',
    a: 'As of 21 November 2026, the known cause is a fault in the SEA-ME-WE 5 (SMW5) submarine cable about 11 km off Pakistan\'s coast, confirmed by the PTA on 18 November. Traffic is being rerouted through other international links, which keeps services running but slows speeds, especially at peak hours. Transworld Associates, which operates the cable landing, expects repairs to finish by 6 December 2026, though that date is tentative.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the slow internet my fault or the network\'s?',
    a: 'Test over an Ethernet cable at a quiet time (early morning). If the wired result is close to your package speed, your line is fine and the problem is Wi-Fi, congestion or the wider network. If wired is also slow at 7 am, the problem is your line or the network, and if neighbours on different providers are slow too, it\'s the network.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is my internet slow only at night?',
    a: 'Evenings (roughly 7 pm to 11 pm) are the busiest hours on any network in Pakistan, everyone is streaming, gaming and on video calls at once. Test in the morning and again at 9 pm: if mornings are fast and evenings are slow, that\'s congestion, and no router setting will fix it. Keep both results as evidence if you complain.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do submarine cable faults happen often in Pakistan?',
    a: 'Often enough to be a recurring problem. Pakistan relies on seven international submarine cables, and faults on one system push extra load onto the rest. The SMW5 cable had a fault in July 2026 as well as the November 2026 fault, both caused nationwide slowdowns while repairs were arranged.',
  },
];

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Guides', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides' },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Why Is the Internet Slow in Pakistan?', item: 'https://ptcl-speed-test.pages.dev/guides/why-internet-slow-pakistan' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Why Is the Internet Slow in Pakistan? SMW5 Fault"
  description="Why is the internet slow in Pakistan? The SMW5 cable fault explained, plus how to tell if it is your line or the network, and what you can do."
  canonical="/guides/why-internet-slow-pakistan"
  schema={[faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]}
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Why Is the Internet Slow?</p>
      <h1>Why Is the Internet Slow in Pakistan?</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        If your internet feels slow this week, there is a known reason, and it probably
        isn't your router. Here's what's happening, how to check whether it's your line
        or the network, and what you can actually do about it.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">Last updated: 21 November 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px;">
      <h2>The current situation: a submarine cable fault</h2>
      <p>
        On Wednesday, 18 November 2026, the Pakistan Telecommunication Authority (PTA)
        confirmed a fault in the SEA-ME-WE 5 (SMW5) international submarine cable, about
        11 kilometres off Pakistan's coast. The PTA warned that users may face
        intermittent connectivity and a decline in service quality while the fault is
        repaired.
      </p>
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>What</th><th>What we know</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Which cable</td><td>SEA-ME-WE 5 (SMW5), landing near Karachi</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fault location</td><td>About 11 km off Pakistan's coast</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Confirmed</td><td>18 November 2026, by the PTA</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Who is fixing it</td><td>Transworld Associates, with the SMW5 consortium, a specialised cable repair ship is needed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Expected repair</td><td>By 6 December 2026, according to Transworld, the date is tentative and could change</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Effect so far</td><td>Slower speeds, especially at peak hours; independent monitors reported a drop in connectivity consistent with the fault</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Traffic is being rerouted through Pakistan's other international links so services
        keep running, but rerouting keeps the lights on, it doesn't keep speeds where
        they were. This is the second fault on the same cable system in 2026: SMW5 also
        failed in July, with similar nationwide effects. Pakistan relies on seven
        submarine cables for international connectivity, so every fault puts extra load
        on the remaining ones.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Sources: PTA statement of 18 November 2026, as reported by The Nation and others;
        Transworld Associates customer notice, as reported by
        <a href="https://www.techjuice.pk/transworld-expects-submarine-cable-repairs-to-finish-by-dec-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TechJuice</a>
        and
        <a href="https://itinsider.pk/pakistan-internet-slow-until-december-6/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ITInsider</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Is it the network or your line?</h2>
      <p>
        Before blaming the cable (or your provider), spend five minutes finding out where
        the problem actually is:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Test over an Ethernet cable.</strong> Plug a laptop into the router and run a <a href="/speed-test">speed test</a>. If the wired result is close to your package speed, your line is fine, the problem is Wi-Fi or congestion.</li>
        <li><strong>Test at a quiet time.</strong> Run the test early in the morning. Fast at 7 am and slow at 9 pm means congestion, not a faulty line.</li>
        <li><strong>Test your mobile data.</strong> If your phone's 4G is also unusually slow, the problem is wider than your home connection.</li>
        <li><strong>Ask a neighbour on a different provider.</strong> If PTCL, Nayatel and Jazz users on your street are all slow, it's the network, no router fix will help.</li>
        <li><strong>Check for outage reports.</strong> Search for the latest PTA notices or check an outage tracker before calling your provider.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>The usual reasons (even without a cable fault)</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Peak-hour congestion (7–11 pm).</strong> Everyone streams at once; the exchange and the international links carry more than they comfortably can.</li>
        <li><strong>Wi-Fi, not the line.</strong> A weak signal or a router in the wrong room can halve your speed. Test wired to see what the line itself delivers.</li>
        <li><strong>Too many devices.</strong> Every phone, TV and laptop shares the same connection, a 10 Mbps line split five ways feels like 2 Mbps each.</li>
        <li><strong>Old copper lines.</strong> On DSL, distance from the exchange and damaged wiring quietly eat speed, and rain makes it worse.</li>
        <li><strong>International links.</strong> Even in normal times, traffic to servers abroad travels through the submarine cables, a fault anywhere on the route shows up as slow, laggy internet at home.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What you can do about it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Test wired, test early, and keep dated screenshots of slow results, they are your evidence.</li>
        <li>Work through the fixes in our <a href="/guides/improve-ptcl-speed">improve your PTCL speed</a> guide and the <a href="/guides/troubleshoot-slow-internet">symptom troubleshooter</a>.</li>
        <li>If your wired speed stays below about 80% of your package at quiet times, register a complaint with your provider and quote the results. For PTCL, the helpline is 1218.</li>
        <li>If it's a cable fault, there is nothing to fix at home, but the tests above tell you that for certain, instead of guessing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <details class="faq">
            <summary>{f.q}</summary>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="cta-band">
        <h2>Is your connection slow right now?</h2>
        <p>Run a test and compare with your package, it takes under 30 seconds.</p>
        <div class="cta-links">
          <a class="btn" href="/speed-test">Test My Speed →</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/statistics">Pakistan speed statistics →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
